      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^{2} - 4 t + 5,\\quad y=- t^{2} - 1,\\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty) ,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Consider each coordinate separately.\n\nFor $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=t^{2} - 4 t + 5 \\\\ &=\\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square takes every nonnegative value. Equality occurs at $t=2$. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded above, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $x\\ge 1$.\n\nFor $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &=- t^{2} - 1 \\\\ &=- t^{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square takes every nonnegative value. Equality occurs at $t=0$. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded below, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $y\\le -1$.\n\nTherefore, $x\\ge 1,\\,y\\le -1$.",
